**Method: Rational Root Theorem (not the divisibility/descent argument).**

Suppose √3 is rational. Then √3 = a/b for integers a, b in lowest terms, so √3 is a root of the polynomial x² − 3 = 0.

By the Rational Root Theorem, any rational root a/b of a monic polynomial with integer coefficients must have b | 1 and a | 3. Hence a/b ∈ {±1, ±3}.

But none of these satisfy x² = 3:
- (±1)² = 1 ≠ 3
- (±3)² = 9 ≠ 3

So x² − 3 has no rational roots, contradicting that √3 is a root. Therefore √3 is irrational.

---

Alternative flavor of "different": using prime exponents. If √3 = a/b, then a² = 3b². In a perfect square, every prime appears with even exponent, so the exponent of 3 in a² is even. But the exponent of 3 in 3b² is 2·v₃(b) + 1, which is odd. Contradiction.
